[s0l0!-header][h1]ES6 Rest Parameters[/h1][/s0l0!-header] 

Prior to ES6, if we wanted to pass a variable number of arguments to a function, we could use the [b]arguments [/b]object, an array-like object, to access the parameters passed to the function.
For example, let's write a function that checks if an array contains all the arguments passed: [code format="js" codeId="1432"]function containsAll(arr) {
  for (let k = 1; k < [b]arguments[/b].length; k++) {
    let num = [b]arguments[/b][k];
    if (arr.indexOf(num) === -1) {
      return false;
    }
  }
  return true;
}
let x = [2, 4, 6, 7];
console.log(containsAll(x, 2, 4, 7));
console.log(containsAll(x, 6, 4, 9));[/code]
We can pass any number of arguments to the function and access it using the [b]arguments [/b]object.

While this does the job, ES6 provides a more readable syntax to achieve variable number of parameters by using a [b]rest parameter[/b]: [code format="js" codeId="1178"]function containsAll(arr, [b]...nums[/b]) {
  for (let num of nums) {
    if (arr.indexOf(num) === -1) {
      return false;
    }
  }
  return true;
}[/code]
The [b]...nums [/b]parameter is called a [b]rest parameter[/b]. It takes all the "extra" arguments passed to the function. The three dots (...) are called the [b]Spread operator[/b].
[note][b][/b]Only the last parameter of a function may be marked as a rest parameter. If there are no extra arguments, the rest parameter will simply be an empty array; the rest parameter will never be [b]undefined[/b].[/note]
