[s0l0!-header][h1]The Spread Operator[/h1][/s0l0!-header] 

This operator is similar to the Rest Parameter, but it has another purpose when used in objects or arrays or function calls (arguments).

[s0l0!-header][h2]Spread in function calls[/h2][/s0l0!-header] 
It is common to pass the elements of an array as arguments to a function. Before ES6, we used the following method:[code format="js" codeId="1930"]function myFunction(w, x, y, z) {
  console.log(w + x + y + z);
}
var [b]args[/b] = [1, 2, 3];
myFunction.apply(null, [b]args.concat[/b](4));[/code]
ES6 provides an easy way to do the example above with [b]spread operators[/b]:[code format="js" codeId="1931"]const myFunction = (w, x, y, z) => {
  console.log(w + x + y + z);
};
let args = [1, 2, 3];
myFunction([b]...args[/b], 4);[/code]
[s0l0!-header][b]Example:[/b][/s0l0!-header][code format="js" codeId="1932"]var dateFields = [1970, 0, 1];  // 1 Jan 1970
var date = new Date([b]...dateFields[/b]);
console.log(date);[/code]
[s0l0!-header][h2]Spread in array literals[/h2][/s0l0!-header]
Before ES6, we used the following syntax to add an item at middle of an array:[code format="js" codeId="1937"]var arr = ["One", "Two", "Five"];

arr.splice(2, 0, "Three");
arr.splice(3, 0, "Four");
console.log(arr);[/code]
You can use methods such as push, splice, and concat, for example, to achieve this in different positions of the array. However, in ES6 the spread operator lets us do this more easily:[code format="js" codeId="1938"]let newArr = ['Three', 'Four']; 
let arr = ['One', 'Two', [b]...newArr[/b], 'Five'];
console.log(arr);[/code]
[s0l0!-header][h2]Spread in object literals[/h2][/s0l0!-header] 
In objects it copies the own enumerable properties from the provided object onto a new object.[code format="js" codeId="1940"]const obj1 = { foo: 'bar', x: 42 };
const obj2 = { foo: 'baz', y: 5 };

const clonedObj = {[b]...obj1[/b]}; // { foo: "bar", x: 42 }
const mergedObj = {[b]...obj1[/b], [b]...obj2[/b]}; // { foo: "baz", x: 42, y: 5 }[/code]
However, if you try to merge them you will not get the result you expected:[code format="js" codeId="1941"]const obj1 = { foo: 'bar', x: 42 };
const obj2 = { foo: 'baz', y: 5 };
const merge = ([b]...objects[/b]) => ({[b]...objects[/b]});

let mergedObj = merge([b]obj1[/b], [b]obj2[/b]);
// { 0: { foo: 'bar', x: 42 }, 1: { foo: 'baz', y: 5 } }

let mergedObj2 = merge([b]{}[/b], [b]obj1[/b], [b]obj2[/b]);
// { 0: {}, 1: { foo: 'bar', x: 42 }, 2: { foo: 'baz', y: 5 } }[/code]
[note]Shallow cloning or merging objects is possible with another operator called [b]Object.assign()[/b].[/note]

